 ## Overview
-Interactive Volume Illustration (Hatching) is an object-space technique for illustrating volumetric data by simulating free-hand line art (hatching) using hardware-assisted volume and line rendering [S2, unofficial][S1]. It highlights curved areas by tracing hatches as polylines over the implicit surface along either a fixed direction or a smoothed principal curvature direction [S1]. It differs from object- or geometry-based silhouette approaches by not extracting edges of a polygonal mesh, but instead computing silhouettes per-fragment for volumetric data using normal maps and view vectors [S2, unofficial].
+Interactive Volume Illustration (Hatching) is an object-space technique for illustrating volumetric data by simulating free-hand line art (hatching) using hardware-assisted volume and line rendering [S2, unofficial][S1]. It highlights curved areas by tracing hatches as polylines over the implicit surface along either a fixed direction or a smoothed principal curvature direction [S1]. Unlike geometry-based silhouette approaches that extract edges from polygonal meshes, this method computes silhouettes and curvature cues per-fragment for volumetric data using normal maps and view vectors, allowing for interactive frame rates on consumer hardware [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in two main phases: stroke generation (preprocessing/runtime) and two-pass rendering [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs:** 3D scalar volume data, transfer function parameters, light direction $L$, view direction $V_{dir}$, user-defined fixed direction $\vec{s}_T$, smoothing weight $w_T$, flatness threshold $\epsilon$, and cross-hatch angle threshold [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Outputs:** A rendered image combining hatching strokes (line strips) and volumetric data (3D texture splatting) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Inputs:** Volumetric scalar field data; user-defined parameters (fixed direction $\vec{s}_T$, flatness threshold $\epsilon$, cross-hatch angle threshold).
+*   **Outputs:** Line-strip primitives representing hatching strokes; rendered image combining hatches and volume rendering.
 
 ### Data Structures
-1.  **Volume Data:** 3D texture map $T_V$ storing scalar values [S2, unofficial].
-2.  **Gradient/Normal Map:** 3D texture map $T_N$ storing normalized gradients $\nabla f_n$ (surface normals) [S2, unofficial].
-3.  **Curvature Data:** Precomputed or on-demand principal curvature directions $\vec{\kappa}_{1j}$ and magnitudes $\kappa_1, \kappa_2$ for seed points [S1].
-4.  **Stroke Buffer:** A texture buffer or vertex buffer storing line-strip primitives [S1][S2, unofficial]. Each stroke consists of a sequence of vertices [S1]. Memory usage is at most one-eighth of the volume data set size [S2, unofficial].
-5.  **Seed Points:** A set of 3D positions representing iso-surfaces, selected at runtime to ensure an optimal number of points [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Seed Points:** A set of 3D positions $(x, y, z)$ selected at runtime to represent iso-surfaces. The number of points is optimized to minimize memory and generation time [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Hatch Strokes:** Stored as line-strip primitives. Each stroke consists of a sequence of vertices. In the tested implementation, the average number of line segments per stroke was 10 [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Usage:** At most one-eighth of the memory consumed by the volume data set is required to store the selected strokes [S2, unofficial].
 
